\f1\fs22 Chanta slowly regained consciousness, his head throbbing in time with the gentle rumble of an engine. He opened his eyes and found himself in the back seat of what appeared to be a meticulously restored 1965 Cadillac Fleetwood, the kind of car that existed only in museums and private collections of the enduringly  wealthy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Ah, the Great Teacher awakens,\'94 said a familiar voice beside him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta turned, blinking away the fog in his vision, and saw The High Elvis sitting next to him in full regalia of white jumpsuit, cape, sunglasses, and that trademark confident smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did you just kidnap me?\'94 Chanta asked, his voice rough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Recruited,\'94 The High Elvis corrected. He gestured out the window. \'93Look at your kingdom, Great Teacher. See what\'92s become of it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta didn\'92t recognise where he was, as if turning a corner had slid the limo into another universe. Every storefront displayed neon crosses and signs promising \'93Salvation Through Song\'94.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the tax happened here?\'94 Chanta needed a bourbon, and possibly an egg and bacon roll.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis held the smile on his face. \'93The government sabotaged our jukeboxes with gospel music. This is the result.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta was confused. \'93They\'92re deliberately voiding warranties?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They are.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They\'92re trying to destabilise us,\'94 The High Elvis grumbled. \'93We\'92ve only just stopped squabbling among ourselves, and now\'85 this.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No one ever takes the Oath of the Warranty seriously though,\'94 Chanta said. Almost every Elvi habitually broke the oath every night and most days.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Except the part about gospel music,\'94 The High Elvis said. \'93Heaven knows why we took that part of the covenant so seriously.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Heaven knows?\'94 Chanta checked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i Ai-ee-yah
\f1\i0 ! Tax! Maybe I\'92m already infected. And I haven\'92t heard any yet!\'94 The High Elvis shimmy-shimmy shook in the back seat of the Cadillac, hoping he could ward off whatever this curse was. \'93In these parts of The Ghetto, you can\'92t drink, dance, or pick a fight without having hymns noodled into your skull.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So we won\'92t be stopping then\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis deflated. \'93There\'92s something I need you to see, Great Teacher.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta waited while The High Elvis chewed over his next words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They\'92ve got a shepherd now,\'94 The High Elvis said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta peered out the window searching for something among the pedestrians, buskers, and garbage bins. \'93How would they even raise sheep here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I mean a religious leader.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i You\'92re
\f1\i0  their leader. They elected you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This guy calls himself Pastor Rex Humble. Up until a few weeks ago, he was just some fan-dude who kept his hair Elvi-style, showed up to all the seediest open-mic nights\'85 you know the type.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The type Chanta knew were the ones who sang karaoke with such commitment, they usually cleared the room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis continued. \'93Now he\'92s got a TV slot where he plays gospel and prays. Out. Loud.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve seen it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, obviously not!\'94 The High Elvis enunciated like a bully with a degree in performance poetry. \'93I\'92ve seen what happens to any Elvi who does.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The limo passed what had been the Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill. Chanta hadn\'92t recognised where they were until he saw its famous neon sign sporting a hastily added \'93& House of Worship\'94 beneath the original name. Armed Elvi guards stood at the entrance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Pull over,\'94 Chanta said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis didn\'92t argue. The Cadillac eased into the kerb with silent obedience.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you coming with?\'94 Chanta asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not a chance,\'94 The High Elvis said. \'93Can\'92t risk it. They say, even if you wear ear plugs, the harmonies vibrate in through your skin.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta doubted that. He shoved the door open and stepped out, blinking against the street air. He couldn\'92t remember it ever stinging so much.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I want a full report,\'94 The High Elvis said, before the car door shut.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The outside of the club looked like someone had stapled a church to its forehead. There were crosses and depictions of three men and a baby Jesus all over it. A queue of Elvi, heads bowed, waited to enter in modest jumpsuits\'97no sequins or rhinestones. None wore a cape.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta swallowed and blinked tears away. 
\f2\i Was the air always this bad?
\f1\i0  As he neared the front of the queue, he could hear a chorus inside singing a flat and pious tune that was far from the rock and roll he\'92d loved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the door a bouncer in a plain black jacket patted everyone down for contraband with the gentle creepiness of a priest copping a feel. Chanta joined the queue. The bouncer\'92s eyes flicked over Chanta\'97up, down, and then to his face, lingering a fraction too long.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No,\'94 the bouncer said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta blinked. \'93No what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No entry.\'94 The bouncer\'92s voice became calm and rehearsed: \'93This place is for those seeking salvation.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m\'97\'94 Chanta started, then stopped himself. He wasn\'92t sure what he was anymore. He sniffled and tried to blink the stinging air out of his eyes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I recognise you, Fake Teacher.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta folded his posture into the shape of penitence. If there was anything he\'92d learned about pious people, it was that they respected compliance almost as much as violence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And now I\'92m seeking salvation,\'94 he said, and it sounded genuine, even to his own ears. He bowed his head. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You smell like cheap bourbon,\'94 the bouncer said, placing his hand on Chanta\'92s shoulder. \'93Not worship.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta was about to correct the bouncer about the price of his bourbon, but choked it back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind the bouncer, someone inside began to speak, voice amplified, the cadence unmistakable: preacher rhythm, showman timing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta took one step sideways, as if being pushed by the queue. He let his weight go slack in his knees, like a man overcome by the hokey spirit, or whatever it was called. He sagged forward with just enough theatricality to be annoying, but not enough to be suspicious. His mouth made a soft sound that could have been a sob, or indigestion. He sang out, \'93Glory, glory, Chattanooga!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi behind him tutted sympathetically, then stepped in to help, hands on Chanta\'92s arms.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bouncer hesitated. Maybe this sinner 
\f2\i had 
\f1\i0 repented. His tears seemed genuine. If he dragged him back out, he might incense the believers, and believers were exhausting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So he let them steer Chanta through the doorway. \'93Saved,\'94 one stated, with the delighted superiority of someone who\'92d found a new way to be better than you.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, the showroom had been rearranged into something that wanted to be a church and couldn\'92t quite forget it was a bar. The booths by the wall were still there, but people only sat facing the stage. All the mirrors and rhinestones had been prised off walls and replaced with cheap wooden crosses. A pile of confiscated items sat on a table near the door\'97hip flasks, sequinned belts, a pair of glitter boots\'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There were no pool tables. Chanta also suspected there were no smashed toilet bowls in the bathrooms. Even worse, he couldn\'92t see evidence of a bullet hole anywhere.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Almost everyone in the room was kneeling in rows facing the stage, above which a light pulsed like a heartbeat. Chanta\'92s handlers pushed him down onto the floor near the back, near the wall, like placing an offering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A preacher\'92s voice rose over the room, engorged with conviction. \'93I speak of 
\f2\i pride
\f1\i0 ,\'94 he thundered, and the crowd shuddered as if pride was a physical contaminant. \'93Pride is the sin that cast Lucifer from heaven!\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta lifted his head just enough to see.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 There are those who are called to preach, those to whom preaching is merely the first thing they were ever good at, and then there are those who are neither, but recognise a good chance to build a TV empire out of it anyway. Pastor Rex Humble was firmly the third, and quickly honed a skill for explaining to camera what is wrong with you and how much the cure will cost. When the schism came and the Ghetto filled with the newly devout, Rex Humble recognised an opening in the market and filled it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Rex Humble stood behind a makeshift pulpit that three weeks ago had been the lectern where a 
\f2\i ma\'eetre d\'92
\f1\i0  turned away tourists in the wrong shoes. Even in the dim punctured by the pulsing stage light, Chanta saw he\'92d modelled himself on the clean-cut Elvi of the early series releases, with Elvi-inspired hair coaxed upward with as much faith as product. Somehow, he still looked like a man beamed in to explain a payment plan, despite the pink suit, and teeth corrected for a camera that wasn\'92t in the room yet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which of my faithful would come next before me and these Biblical Shears?\'94 Humble called to his congregation. \'93For nowhere in His holy word did the good Lord reach for electric clippers.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An Elvine kneeling near the stage stood and approached the pulpit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93With this blessing, do you renounce the pride of both sideburns 
\f2\i and
\f1\i0  a Number Three guard comb?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvine nodded and knelt. Humble ceremoniously lifted his \'93biblical shears\'94 while the crowd sang a hymn with pelvis-rigid solemnity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta snorted. 
\f2\i The title was too obvious
\f1\i0 , he thought. 
\f2\i It should have been called \'93Amazing Grass\'94.\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How sweet the smoke,\'94 Chanta sang to himself. \'93That baked a lech like me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Not his best material, he had to admit. He wasn\'92t even sure if 
\f2\i lech 
\f1\i0 was a word.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Humble kissed the blades of his \'93shears\'94\'97which were, in fact, orange-handled stationery scissors he had bought years ago at Officejerks and recently stopped using to open bags of Cocoa Pops. He held them high like the jawbone of an ass, paused for effect, then placed their open blades against the jaw of the Elvine supplicant.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Humble snipped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sideburn hair fell into Humble\'92s cupped hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The kneeling Elvine flinched as if she\'92d been hit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Humble let the hair fall into a cut-glass punch bowl on the pulpit\'97the last survivor of several identical bowls gifted to his great-grandparents on the happy occasion of their shotgun wedding. It was already half-filled with dark curls like some relic dust-bunny.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta wanted to start a fight he couldn\'92t win, and might have, except there were no pool cues in the club any more. Not even a black ball he could throw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stood and forced himself to leisurely walk to the exit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, ye of little faith!\'94 Humble called to Chanta\'92s back. Chanta turned and rubbed an index finger to his cheek, calling attention to his smooth face; he couldn\'92t grow credible sideburns for tax, and it wasn\'92t for lack of trying.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On stage, Humble raised his hands and the crowd rose with him, swaying in unison, a sea of obedience.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta fled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Outside, the air was clearer, but not kinder.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He stood on the footpath, staring at the sign: Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill & House of Worship. As if you could just bolt a religion onto a building and call it salvation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He swallowed bile and rage. It stayed down, but only just.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Back in the limo, Chanta hissed, barely moving his lips, the rage still riding him. \'93This is\'85\'94 and could not find the words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Contagion,\'94 The High Elvis murmured back. \'93It spreads among Elvi by song. By repetition. By being in the room.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m glad you didn\'92t come in.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92d been forewarned.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They rode in silence, all the way back to Starborne One. The limo passed through its sally port to the motor lobby.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, thanks for the tour,\'94 Chanta said as pulled the latch for the door. It was locked. \'93Um, are we making out or something?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you with us, Great Teacher?\'94 The High Elvis asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93With you. Always. I love you guys.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i You
\f1\i0  guys,\'94 The High Elvis repeated and pondered. \'93We consider you one of us.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Really?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But you\'92ve changed. Being rich is ruining you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta shrugged. \'93It ruins everyone, I guess.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis looked out the window, across the footpath, and into the opulent lobby. \'93Invite me in.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s mind went blank, as it often did, especially when he was sober. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do I need another pool cue?\'94 The High Elvis threatened.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta laughed. \'93That\'92s a good idea. Let\'92s both grab a cue.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta got lost trying to find the games room in the penthouse, and had to call for Fisher to guide them. They were on the wrong floor.\
