\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Chanta let The High Elvis win the first five or six games, then gradually let the score even up, though he never let himself get ahead. He wasn\'92t sure The High Elvis was counting; they\'92d both consumed peanut-butter-based cocktails at the rate of about one every three or four games. Chanta couldn\'92t taste the alcohol in them and knew that was the point.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You hungry?\'94 Chanta asked after The High Elvis sunk the nine-ball from where Chanta had left to for him, and moved three ahead in the score.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sure.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta called the kitchen and ordered fried chicken. They took it at the bar and hunkered in. Chanta drank beer and The High Elvis drank Pepsi that Ross the sommelier had to order in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I want to talk about how we fight this gospel music epidemic,\'94 The High Elvis said after finishing a fourth piece of chicken and wiping grease from his mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sure.\'94 Chanta kept eating.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This is a war of memes,\'94 The High Elvis said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta nodded because he had no idea what a meme was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have a plan,\'94 The High Elvis said, and Chanta could instantly tell from his change in voice, this was something he\'92d rehearsed. \'93We\'92ve learned that the only way to fight music is with music. So let\'92s answer them with a festival. Something the Elvi have never staged before. Stream it live, so it reaches every last Elvi, and the uncloned along with them. I want to appeal to everyone to help us end the debilitating scourge of gospel music.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta admired the scale of it, even though he couldn\'92t really scale the scale of it. \'93Okay,\'94 was all he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m calling it the Rock Against Revival,\'94 The High Elvis went on. \'93Like the biggest Vegas Show in history. Open-air, over a whole weekend, not just the one night.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sounds awesome,\'94 Chanta agreed and took a swig from his bottle of Platypiss Lager. Chanta had developed a taste for expensive and endangered craft brews. Chanta gave this brand about six months.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Graceland won\'92t hold the crowd I\'92m hoping for. I think we have to go outside The Ghetto for this. Outside the Pale.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta was nodding. He thought of those Carols by Candlelight and classical music concerts under the stars the Airborne loved so much. They were among the  few times they forgot that the ground might give them germs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hold it in The Domain,\'94 Chanta said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis straightened on his barstool. \'93Exactly what I\'92m planning, Great Teacher.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta laughed, once, before he understood The High Elvis wasn\'92t joking. At the western end of the Eastern Suburbs anti-climate bubble, The Domain was thirty or forty hectares of the most nervously tended lawn in the country where the Airborne meritocracy held its galas, executions and, in a good month, both\'97often at Christmas and New Year. \'93I want to hold it where no one can look away, neither those Holy Roller Elvi or the Airborne,\'94 The High Elvis said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sounds like a great idea,\'94 Chanta said and drained his beer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Chanta said nothing more, The High Elvis finally got to the point. \'93We need your help.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stood and went around the bar to the over-sized fridge. \'93You want another?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis nodded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Chanta put his butt back on the bar stool and taken a gentle sip, he asked warily, \'93You want money?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, Great Teacher. Your songs have already brought great wealth to the Elvi. They\'92re even played on Airborne college radio stations.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There it is
\f1\i0 , Chanta thought. The High Elvis wanted to sell tickets to Airborne kids. Elvi music was everywhere now, even in a place called Thailand where they were called \'93Cat Kings\'94.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What we need,\'94 The High Elvis pressed, \'93is \'85 
\f2\i influence
\f1\i0 .\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m no influencer,\'94 Chanta said flatly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m sure there\'92s more than one person on your payroll who could find the 
\f2\i influence
\f1\i0  we need. Your accountants, your lawyers, that terrifying woman who runs your diary. You have money, Great Teacher. Historic, forget-where-you-left-it money. And money that old, I imagine, buys the one thing we can\'92t: people who make paperwork disappear on your behalf. I\'92d guess you have a shelf company somewhere that could lodge the permit application. You\'92d call it a classical concert playing the composers everyone hates\'85 the ones who write the movie soundtracks that put you to sleep. That ought to suit the Domain Trust well enough, since they\'92d still get their booking fee and expect next to no one would show up. Your company tells them it\'92s a tax deduction, and everyone waves it through. Or so I imagine. I\'92ve never done any of this.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How would you sell tickets? I mean to anyone outside The Pale?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92re selling plenty of vinyl all over the country right now. We\'92ll slip a free ticket inside each dust jacket with a web  address for buying more.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta wondered if that would work, whether they\'92d sell enough tickets without the wrong people finding out. \'93How many people can fit on The Domain?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis shrugged. \'93Half a million, I guess. With that and live streaming, we could reprogram thousands of Holy Rollers 
\f2\i and
\f1\i0  shove a drumstick up those Airborne noses.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta thought about Humble's punch bowl of hairballs, and the way the whole room had swayed, and how close he had come to swaying with it. 
\f2\i Fight music with music.
\f1\i0  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta never backed away from a fight.
